        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Stainless steel and induction are a great match when the base is magnetic, flat and sized to the zone. The grade on the box is a clue, not an answer; a fridge magnet held to the center of the base is the answer. Firm grip means it will work, a faint grip means it will struggle, and no grip means it needs a converter or a new home.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are replacing pans anyway, fully clad stainless with a magnetic exterior is the most versatile thing you can buy for an induction hob. Start with the pieces you use every day, keep the ones that pass, and do not pay for a full set just to replace the two pans that failed.
          </p>
